  "author": "",
  "attributionNote": "The book is anonymous. Its author, a 14th-century English priest writing for a young disciple, is unknown; the Cloud has been attributed to Walter Hilton and to other contemplatives, but no attribution is secure.",
  "description": "The Cloud of Unknowing is the classic English manual of contemplative prayer: a series of short chapters teaching the disciple to forget all creatures and all thoughts and to press into the cloud of unknowing with a blind stirring of love. It is practical and unsentimental, warning against false fervour, bodily techniques and the presumption of beginners, and its image of the cloud, taken from Dionysius, became the central metaphor of Western apophatic spirituality. The volume also carries the author's prayer and prologue.",
  "historicalContext": "The Cloud belongs to the flowering of English vernacular mysticism in the late 14th century, alongside the writings of Julian of Norwich and Walter Hilton. Evelyn Underhill's 1922 edition modernised the Middle English spelling and added an introduction that made the book a founding text of the modern contemplative revival.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete. The prayer, the prologue and all seventy-five chapters are streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time, one chapter per chapter. The edition's introduction, glossary and table of chapters are not part of the work, and the modernised Middle English of Underhill's text is reproduced as the archive serves it.",
